--TEST--
Core\Password::verify and ::needsRehash refuse a stored value that is not a hash this class wrote, and verify caps the memory a stored hash may ask for — spec § 16, `rule:core-api/shape-rules`, `rule:security/sink-predicate`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// PHP answers `false` from `password_verify` and `true` from
// `password_needs_rehash` for a malformed hash, which reads at the login
// screen exactly like every user typing the wrong password at once. `rule:core-api/shape-rules`'s
// "failure throws" is why both throw here instead; `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/password.rs`
// is the home of that divergence. The messages are asserted whole because the
// one thing they must never contain is the stored value itself.
secret string $password = "correct horse battery staple";

try {
    bool $answered = Core\Password::verify($password, "not a stored hash");
    echo "verified against a non-hash", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $notAHash) {
    echo $notAHash->message, "\n";
}

try {
    bool $stale = Core\Password::needsRehash("not a stored hash");
    echo "measured a non-hash", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $notMeasurable) {
    echo $notMeasurable->message, "\n";
}

// A PHC string carries its own cost, and `verify` honours it so that a hash
// written under older parameters still verifies. That makes the stored column
// a lever on this process's memory — one row asking for 2 GiB — so there is a
// ceiling, and it refuses before anything is allocated.
string $greedy = '$argon2id$v=19$m=2097152,t=2,p=1$jJ+hokoSJRsAzYsgfwhV6g$BGyWXoH11l0/GF4ezLqvtQgPY0T/4fv4DukErq9R0cI';
try {
    bool $ruinous = Core\Password::verify($password, $greedy);
    echo "honoured a 2 GiB memory cost out of the store", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $tooGreedy) {
    echo $tooGreedy->message, "\n";
}

// A well-formed PHC string naming something else is the same storage bug one
// layer in, so it is the same class of refusal rather than a `false`.
string $foreign = '$pbkdf2$v=19$m=19456,t=2,p=1$jJ+hokoSJRsAzYsgfwhV6g$BGyWXoH11l0/GF4ezLqvtQgPY0T/4fv4DukErq9R0cI';
try {
    bool $checked = Core\Password::verify($password, $foreign);
    echo "checked a password against another algorithm", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $notOurs) {
    echo $notOurs->message, "\n";
}

// The other side of the bound: the value this class did write is read back
// without complaint, so the four refusals above are about the stored value and
// not about `verify` having become a member that refuses everything.
string $ours = Core\Password::hash($password);
if (Core\Password::verify($password, $ours)) {
    echo "and the hash this class wrote is read back", "\n";
} else {
    echo "refused its own hash", "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
Core\Password::verify(): $hash is not a stored password hash — the PHC string Core\Password::hash() writes was expected. The value is not quoted here, because a stored hash does not belong in a log.
Core\Password::needsRehash(): $hash is not a stored password hash — the PHC string Core\Password::hash() writes was expected. The value is not quoted here, because a stored hash does not belong in a log.
Core\Password::verify(): $hash asks for 2097152 KiB of memory, past this class's 1048576 KiB ceiling — no hash it writes needs that
Core\Password::verify(): $hash parses as a PHC string but is not one Core\Password::hash() wrote — the algorithm, the version or the salt is not this class's.
and the hash this class wrote is read back
